lessons=[dict(id='encoder',title='Read first. Then write.',tag='ENCODER–DECODER ARCHITECTURE',hook='Three English words become two French tokens. Which output belongs to which input clock?',left='I → love → you',right='je → t’aime → EOS',problem=['Read three source positions','Carry a source summary','Generate until an end token'],caption='A token is a unit chosen by the tokenizer, not necessarily a whole word. Here t’aime is one teaching token; EOS is an extra control token.',explain='''<p>A <b>sequence</b> is an ordered collection: words, audio frames, sensor measurements or video frames. Order matters: “dog bites person” and “person bites dog” contain the same words but tell different stories. A token ID is mapped to an <b>embedding vector</b>; its integer ID is not a meaningful numerical magnitude.</p><p>A recurrent encoder reads x₁ … xₙ, updating a <b>hidden state hᵢ</b> with the same learned rule at each position. The state is a changing summary; the weights are the learned rule. A separate decoder writes y₁ … yₘ, one target token at a time. This handles unequal lengths and output reordering. Encoding and decoding have separate parameter sets.</p><div class="analogy"><p>Imagine a reader writing a briefing note, then handing it to a translator. The reader’s clock counts source positions; the translator’s clock counts output positions. They do not have to tick together.</p></div><p>In the baseline below, the final encoder state c initializes the decoder through a learned bridge. At inference, the decoder starts with <b>BOS</b>, feeds its own last prediction back, and stops on <b>EOS</b> or a length limit. During training, <b>teacher forcing</b> instead supplies the correct previous target.</p>''',formula='hᵢ = Encoder(hᵢ₋₁, Eₓ(xᵢ)); c = hₙ<br>s₀ = bridge(c); sₜ = Decoder(sₜ₋₁, Eᵧ(yₜ₋₁))<br>p(yₜ | y&lt;ₜ, x) = softmax(Wo sₜ + bo)',legend='i indexes source positions; t indexes target positions. E maps a token to a vector. h and s are encoder and decoder states. This baseline conditions on c through s₀; other baselines also inject c at each decoder step.',deep_title='Start here if RNNs, dimensions or training are new',deep='''<p>A simple recurrent cell is hᵢ = tanh(Wₓxᵢ + Wₕhᵢ₋₁ + b). For input width d and hidden width H, Wₓ is H × d, Wₕ is H × H and b is H × 1. Tanh acts coordinate by coordinate. A sequence of 4 inputs or 40 inputs still produces H coordinates per state. Practical encoders often use LSTM/GRU cells, but attention does not require knowing their gates.</p><p>For encoder width Hₑ and decoder width H𝒹, a bridge can be s₀ = tanh(Wbridge c + bbridge), with Wbridge of shape H𝒹 × Hₑ. An LSTM has a cell state too; a bridge must initialize all required recurrent states.</p><table class="math-table"><caption>Teacher forcing shifts the target; the answer never becomes its own input.</caption><tr><th>Target step</th><th>Decoder input</th><th>Training target</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>BOS</td><td>je</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>je</td><td>t’aime</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>t’aime</td><td>EOS</td></tr></table><p>The model’s probability for a whole output is ∏ₜ p(yₜ | y&lt;ₜ, x). Training minimizes the sum of negative log probabilities of correct targets, excluding padded target positions. Teacher forcing makes training easier but creates a mismatch: inference must cope with its own mistakes. Greedy decoding takes the highest probability token each step; beam search keeps several partial outputs. Neither guarantees the best translation.</p>''',tryit='Switch from teacher forcing to inference. A scripted first error (“tu”) changes the next decoder input. Explain why the same training target cannot be supplied at inference.'),
dict(id='bottleneck',title='A whole sentence.<br>One fixed briefing note.',tag='CONTEXT VECTOR BOTTLENECK',hook='The translator may need the first name much later. What if every detail has to travel through one final summary?',left='A short sentence → c ∈ ℝᴴ',right='A long sentence → c ∈ ℝᴴ',problem=['Early clue enters','Many recurrent rewrites','One summary serves all outputs'],caption='Increasing source length increases the number of recurrent steps. It does not automatically increase the final vector width.',explain='''<p>The baseline asks one fixed-width vector to preserve whatever every future output might need. As source sequences become more complex, that is a demanding representation and optimization problem. Useful details may become hard to recover, and a late training error must influence early recurrent computations through a long path.</p><div class="analogy"><p>A briefing note is convenient, but a translator may want to reopen the original notebook to check a name. Making the note larger can help; giving the translator access to all the notes changes the information route.</p></div><p>The experiment compares two sequences differing only in their first scalar input. Repeated updates can make their final summaries similar. This is an illustration of <b>sensitivity loss</b>, not a measured accuracy curve and not a claim that an H-dimensional state stores exactly H words.</p><p>The way out is to keep the encoder’s intermediate states H = [h₁, …, hₙ]. For each target step, retrieve a new context cₜ from this bank. The retrieved vector still has fixed width, but it no longer has to be the same summary for every output.</p>''',formula='Baseline: c = hₙ for the whole output<br>Attention: cₜ = Σᵢ αₜᵢ hᵢ; Σᵢ αₜᵢ = 1<br>Toy experiment: hᵢ = tanh(xᵢ + r hᵢ₋₁)',legend='The toy starts from h₀ = 0. Compare x₁ = +1 versus −1; all later inputs are zero. r is a scalar retention coefficient. The real architecture uses vectors and learned recurrent matrices.',deep_title='Capacity, gradient paths and the cost of keeping notes',deep='''<p>A real-valued vector has no literal fixed “word count.” Finite precision, learned features, data and optimization all affect which distinctions survive. A wider state or a gated recurrence can help; attention provides an additional route rather than proving those alternatives useless.</p><p>For this scalar recurrence, ∂hₙ/∂h₁ = ∏ᵢ₌₂ⁿ r(1 − hᵢ²). With |r| &lt; 1, this product can shrink rapidly. Vector RNNs involve products of Jacobian matrices, which can shrink or grow. This is related to training gradients, but the plotted final-state difference is not itself a gradient measurement.</p><p>A source bank stores n × Hₑ numbers instead of just Hₑ. Global encoder–decoder attention compares all n source positions for each of m target steps, giving n × m score evaluations. Projection and recurrent computation add their own costs. Attention improves access to source states; it does not guarantee exact copying, perfect long-range memory or correct translation.</p>''',tryit='Reveal the final summary, then increase length from 3 to 12. Compare r = 0.5 and r = 1.0. State exactly what this toy experiment demonstrates—and what it cannot establish.'),
dict(id='recipe',title='Which note matters<br>for this word?',tag='THE SHARED ATTENTION RECIPE',hook='When writing “je”, should the translator look at the same source evidence as when writing “t’aime”?',left='One fixed context c',right='A fresh context c₁, c₂, …',problem=['Query: what do I need?','Score every source state','Normalize → weighted mixture'],caption='The query comes from the decoder. Keys are source representations used for scoring; values are the source vectors that get mixed. In these classical examples, the same hᵢ supplies both source roles.',explain='''<p>Attention is a differentiable retrieval operation. First, compare the decoder <b>query</b> with every source state to produce <b>scores eₜᵢ</b>. Second, apply softmax <em>across source positions</em> to obtain weights αₜᵢ. Third, multiply each entire source vector by its scalar weight and add the vectors to obtain <b>context cₜ</b>.</p><div class="analogy"><p>A spotlight can illuminate several notes at once. The brightnesses add to one, and the reader combines the illuminated information. A moving spotlight is not a command to copy exactly one note.</p></div><p>The three source vectors below are deliberately hand-chosen: h₁ = [1, 0], h₂ = [0, 1], h₃ = [1, 1]. With q = [1, 0] and dot scoring, scores are [1, 0, 1], weights are approximately [0.422, 0.155, 0.422], and c ≈ [0.845, 0.578]. The decoder still needs a vocabulary head to predict the next token.</p>''',formula='eₜᵢ = score(qₜ, hᵢ)<br>αₜᵢ = exp(eₜᵢ) / Σⱼ exp(eₜⱼ)<br>cₜ = Σᵢ αₜᵢ hᵢ',legend='For n source positions, eₜ and αₜ each contain n scalars. If hᵢ has Hₑ coordinates, cₜ also has Hₑ coordinates. The weights are source-position probabilities, not vocabulary probabilities.',deep_title='Worked arithmetic, padding and reading a heatmap',deep='''<p>exp([1, 0, 1]) = [2.718, 1, 2.718]; the denominator is 6.437. The first context coordinate is 0.422 × 1 + 0.155 × 0 + 0.422 × 1 ≈ 0.845. The second is 0.422 × 0 + 0.155 × 1 + 0.422 × 1 ≈ 0.578. Displayed values are rounded; calculations use full precision.</p><p>Stable softmax subtracts the largest valid score before exponentiating. Adding the same constant to every score leaves the weights unchanged. All equal scores give a uniform distribution. Padding is masked before normalization (conceptually score = −∞), giving zero weight; otherwise a fake position can steal probability mass. At least one source position must be valid.</p><p>The temperature slider uses softmax(e/T). Lower T sharpens existing preferences; it cannot choose between an exact tie. T = 1 is the classical formula used here. This is an experimental control, not a necessary part of Bahdanau or Luong attention.</p><p>Read a heatmap across one row: a single target step distributes a total of one unit over source positions. Columns need not sum to one. Alignments may be diffuse, reordered, repeated or many-to-one. Bright cells can be useful diagnostics, but attention weights alone are not proof of a linguistic alignment or a causal explanation.</p>''',tryit='Change the target-step preset, then edit either query coordinate. Mask the third source row and check that the other weights renormalize. Explain why every context coordinate changes together.'),
dict(id='additive',title='Learn a small network<br>that compares two states.',tag='BAHDANAU / ADDITIVE ATTENTION',hook='The encoder writes 4 numbers per position. The decoder asks with 3. How can we compare vectors with different widths?',left='Decoder q ∈ ℝ³',right='Source hᵢ ∈ ℝ⁴',problem=['Project both to width A','Add → tanh → learned v','One compatibility score'],caption='A raw dot product needs matching widths. Separate projections can put different-width source and query vectors into a common scoring space.',explain='''<p>Bahdanau attention learns a small feedforward scoring network. It separately projects the previous decoder state and each encoder annotation, adds the results, applies tanh, then combines the coordinates with a learned vector v. “Additive” names the addition inside this scoring function; it does not mean adding raw words or adding unnormalized probabilities.</p><div class="analogy"><p>Two specialists submit reports in different formats. A learned rubric converts both reports into comparable criteria, then combines the criteria into one relevance score.</p></div><p>In the familiar Bahdanau ordering, <b>sₜ₋₁ queries the source before the new decoder state sₜ is formed</b>. The context then helps the decoder update and predict. The original model uses a bidirectional encoder, so each source annotation includes information from both directions. Our two-coordinate bank keeps arithmetic visible.</p>''',formula='eₜᵢ = vᵀ tanh(Wq sₜ₋₁ + Wh hᵢ + b)<br>αₜ = softmax(eₜ); cₜ = Σᵢ αₜᵢ hᵢ<br>sₜ = Decoder(sₜ₋₁, Eᵧ(yₜ₋₁), cₜ)',legend='Wq: A × H𝒹; Wh: A × Hₑ; b and v: A × 1. A is the attention-network width. The context uses original hᵢ, so its width is Hₑ, not A. A vocabulary head reads the resulting decoder information.',deep_title='A full scored pair, parameter shapes and the original model',deep='''<p>For the interactive toy, Wq = Wh = [[1,0],[0,1]], b = [0,0], v = [1,−1], q = [1,0]. For h₁ = [1,0], the sum is [2,0], tanh gives [0.964,0], and e₁ = 0.964. For h₂ = [0,1], e₂ = tanh(1) − tanh(1) = 0. For h₃ = [1,1], e₃ = tanh(2) − tanh(1) ≈ 0.202. Softmax then produces the weights; a tanh output or a raw score alone is not a weight.</p><p>With H𝒹 = 3, Hₑ = 4, A = 5: Wq has 15 parameters, Wh has 20, v has 5, and optional b has 5, for 45 scoring parameters with this bias convention. The original paper writes its alignment function without the explicit bias shown in this common equivalent teaching form.</p><p>All scorer parameters learn through the translation loss; the standard soft attention mechanism does not require a separate hand-labelled alignment target. The original decoder has a richer recurrent/output construction than this high-level schematic. Source annotations may concatenate forward and backward hidden states, doubling the annotation width if each direction has the same width.</p>''',tryit='Use q = [1,0]. Trace h₁ through projection, addition, tanh and v; then reveal the weights. Change q to [0,1] and compare the ranking. The toy weights need not form a sensible translation alignment.'),
dict(id='multiplicative',title='Multiply to compare.<br>Keep the retrieval recipe.',tag='LUONG / MULTIPLICATIVE ATTENTION',hook='Do we need a little nonlinear network for every pair—or can multiplication give us a useful compatibility score?',left='Add → tanh → v',right='Multiply → sum',problem=['Update decoder state sₜ','Dot or learned bilinear score','Retrieve cₜ → combine → predict'],caption='The classical Luong global formulation computes the current decoder state first, then attends over all valid source positions.',explain='''<p>Luong attention offers several scoring functions. The <b>dot</b> form takes sₜᵀhᵢ; it has no scoring parameters and requires matching widths. The <b>general</b> form takes sₜᵀWa hᵢ; its learned matrix can map source width to decoder width. These are the multiplicative forms explored here. The paper also describes a concat scorer.</p><div class="analogy"><p>A dot product scores agreement between coordinates. A learned matrix changes the measuring instrument first: it can amplify, mix or reverse which source features matter to the query.</p></div><p>After computing scores, the same softmax and weighted sum retrieve cₜ. Luong combines cₜ with the current state sₜ to form an <b>attentional state</b>, which feeds the vocabulary head. Input feeding can also pass the previous attentional state into the next decoder step.</p><p>Both attention families let a decoder query an encoder memory bank. Score functions and decoder update order are useful distinctions; “additive” versus “multiplicative” alone does not dictate every architectural choice in later implementations.</p>''',formula='Dot: eₜᵢ = sₜᵀhᵢ<br>General: eₜᵢ = sₜᵀWa hᵢ<br>cₜ = Σᵢ softmax(eₜ)ᵢ hᵢ<br>s̃ₜ = tanh(Wc [cₜ; sₜ]); p(yₜ) = softmax(Wo s̃ₜ + bo)',legend='For column vectors, Wa is H𝒹 × Hₑ. [cₜ; sₜ] means concatenation, not addition. This is classical unscaled scoring; the √d scaling used in Transformer attention is not inserted here.',deep_title='Worked general score, ordering and computational tradeoffs',deep='''<p>With q = [1,1] and Wa = [[1,0],[0,2]], the transformed sources are [1,0], [0,2], [1,2]; general scores are [1,2,3]. Dot scores for the same query are [1,1,2]. The larger second-coordinate multiplier changes the source ranking. At q = [1,0], both scorers happen to agree—one input cannot establish equivalence.</p><p>Dot scoring costs roughly O(H) per source/query pair. General scoring can precompute Wa hᵢ for the source bank. Additive scoring can likewise precompute Wh hᵢ, then adds and applies tanh per pair. Runtime depends on dimensions and implementation; neither family is universally better. Global attention uses all valid source positions. Luong also proposes local attention over a selected window; that is outside this experiment.</p><table class="math-table"><caption>Classic ordering conventions</caption><tr><th></th><th>Bahdanau</th><th>Luong</th></tr><tr><th>Query</th><td>Previous sₜ₋₁</td><td>Current sₜ</td></tr><tr><th>Order</th><td>Attend → update</td><td>Update → attend</td></tr><tr><th>Score here</th><td>vᵀ tanh(Wq q + Wh h)</td><td>qᵀh or qᵀWa h</td></tr><tr><th>Shared operation</th><td colspan="2">Source softmax → weighted context → target prediction</td></tr></table><p>This is encoder–decoder attention: queries come from the target side and memory from the source side. Transformer cross-attention preserves that broad role distinction but changes projections, scaling and architecture. Transformer self-attention compares positions within one sequence. Those are the next chapter, not hidden prerequisites today.</p>''',tryit='Set q = [1,1], compare dot and general scores, then switch back to [1,0]. Explain why the scoring rules agree for one query and disagree for another.')]
# q: prompt, expected, choices (or numeric), explanation
questions=[
('Three source tokens require exactly three generated tokens before EOS.','no',[('yes','True'),('no','False')],'False. Source and target lengths may differ; the decoder generates until EOS or a length limit.'),
('During teacher forcing, what is the input when the training target is t’aime?','je',[('je','The previous correct target: je'),('same','The current correct target: t’aime'),('future','EOS')],'The previous correct target je is supplied. Supplying t’aime to predict itself would leak the answer.'),
('Encoder width is 4 and decoder width is 3. How many rows does a linear bridge Wbridge need?',3,None,'Three rows: a 3 × 4 matrix maps a 4-coordinate context into a 3-coordinate decoder state.'),
('Increase source length from 5 to 50 while keeping hidden width H = 8. How many coordinates remain in the final encoder state?',8,None,'Eight. More recurrent steps do not change hidden width or add independent parameter banks.'),
('What does the scalar bottleneck experiment establish?','toy',[('limit','Every RNN stores at most one word'),('toy','Early distinctions can shrink under the displayed recurrence'),('accuracy','Attention always improves accuracy by a known percentage')],'It demonstrates sensitivity loss for one chosen recurrence. It does not measure translation accuracy or prove a word-storage limit.'),
('What changes when the decoder can attend to all source states?','route',[('route','It gains a fresh route to source evidence per output'),('width','Every context must have nH coordinates'),('copy','It must copy exactly one source word')],'Attention retrieves a new H-dimensional mixture per target step from the retained n-state bank.'),
('For weights [0.25, 0.75] and states [[2,0],[0,4]], what is the second coordinate of c?',3,None,'0.25 × 0 + 0.75 × 4 = 3. The full context is [0.5,3].'),
('Three valid source scores are all equal. What weight does EACH source receive? Enter a decimal or fraction.',1/3,None,'Softmax gives equal weights 1/3. A common score offset does not change these proportions.'),
('A 2 × 3 target-to-source attention matrix has rows that sum to one. Must its columns also sum to one?','no',[('yes','Yes'),('no','No')],'No. Each target step normalizes independently over source positions; different target steps may revisit the same source.'),
('H𝒹 = 3, Hₑ = 4, attention width A = 5. How many rows does Wh have?',5,None,'Five. Wh is 5 × 4; Wq is 5 × 3. Both projections must enter the same 5-coordinate scoring space.'),
('In the classic Bahdanau order, which decoder state queries the source?','previous',[('previous','The previous state sₜ₋₁'),('current','The already-updated state sₜ'),('target','The correct current target word')],'The previous decoder state queries the source, then the context helps form the next decoder state.'),
('If the additive scoring width is 5 but each original source state has width 4, what is the context width?',4,None,'Four. The weights mix original source annotations, not their five-coordinate scoring projections.'),
('For q = [1,1], h = [0,1] and Wa = [[1,0],[0,2]], what is qᵀWa h?',2,None,'Wa h = [0,2], and [1,1] · [0,2] = 2. The raw dot score would be 1.'),
('Which operation is required after computing source attention weights?','mix',[('copy','Return the source token with maximum weight as the target'),('mix','Mix source states, then use decoder information and a vocabulary head'),('sort','Sort the output words by source weight')],'A weighted context is evidence for a target prediction. It is not the prediction itself.'),
('The dot scorer has no learned scoring matrix. Does this mean the whole encoder–decoder is untrained?','no',[('yes','Yes'),('no','No')],'No. The encoder, decoder, embeddings and output head still have learned parameters. General scoring additionally learns Wa.'),
('A source padding position has been masked. What attention weight should it receive?',0,None,'Exactly zero, with the other valid positions renormalized to sum to one.'),
('Choose the correct classic ordering comparison.','order',[('order','Bahdanau: attend then update; Luong: update then attend'),('reverse','Bahdanau: update then attend; Luong: attend then update'),('identical','Both always attend at an identical point in every implementation')],'This comparison describes the classic formulations used here. Later implementations can combine scorers and recurrent update arrangements differently.'),
('An attention heatmap looks diagonal. What can we safely conclude?','weights',[('cause','It proves the model reasons exactly like a human'),('perfect','Every translation is correct'),('weights','For these inputs, each target step gives relatively high weight near the corresponding source position')],'The heatmap reports weights for the displayed computation. Linguistic correctness and causal explanation require additional evidence.')]
def checks(start,count=3):
 out=''
 for idx in range(start,start+count):
  q,answer,options,ex=questions[idx]; n=idx+1
  fields=(f'<fieldset><legend>Choose one answer</legend>'+''.join(f'<label><input type="radio" name="answer" value="{v}"> {t}</label>' for v,t in options)+'</fieldset>') if options else f'<label for="a{n}">Your numerical answer</label><input type="text" id="a{n}" name="answer" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Number or fraction">'
  out+=f'<form class="check" id="q{n}" data-answer="{answer}" data-kind="{"choice" if options else "number"}"><span class="qnum">CHECK {n:02d}</span><h3>{q}</h3>{fields}<div class="check-actions"><button type="submit">Check answer</button><span class="feedback" role="status"></span></div><details class="answer-fold"><summary>Reveal worked reasoning</summary><p>{ex}</p></details></form>'
 return out
