page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="A beginner lecture worksheet on encoder–decoder architectures, the context bottleneck, Bahdanau additive and Luong multiplicative attention. Five guided animations and eighteen worked checks."><title>Encoder–decoder & attention · Look back for the next word</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"><script defer src="model.js"></script><script defer src="../sequence-modelling/timeline.js"></script><script defer src="storyboards.js"></script><script defer src="scenes.js"></script><script defer src="app.js"></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to worksheet</a><header class="topbar"><a class="brand" href="../../index.html"><span class="logo">λ</span><span>DEEP LEARNING<small>Presentation & practice sheets</small></span></a><div class="progress-wrap"><span id="progress-label">0 of 18 checks answered correctly</span><progress id="progress" value="0" max="18" aria-label="Checks answered correctly"></progress></div><button id="print">Print worksheet</button></header><div class="layout"><aside class="sidebar"><span class="eyebrow">SEQUENCE MODELLING / 03</span><nav aria-label="Worksheet sections">'''
page+=''.join(f'<a href="#{i}"><span>{n:02d}</span>{t}</a>' for n,(i,t) in enumerate(nav))
page+='''</nav><div class="sidebar-note">Read the whole sentence.<br>Look back for the next word.</div><a class="asset-link" href="instructor-guide.md">Instructor guide ↗</a><a class="asset-link" href="assets/gallery.html">Reusable SVG figures ↗</a><a class="asset-link" href="../sequence-modelling/">Sequence foundations ↗</a></aside><main id="main" class="prose"><div class="intro"><a class="back" href="../../index.html">← All worksheets</a><div class="eyebrow">LECTURE PRESENTATION / ENCODER–DECODER · ATTENTION</div><h1>Read the whole sentence.<br><em>Look back for<br>the next word.</em></h1><p class="lede">A translator reads a sentence once. Must every detail fit in one memory? Today, we build a reader, a writer, and a way for the writer to look back.</p><div class="pills"><span>100–120 minutes</span><span>5 guided animations</span><span>18 checks + design challenge</span><span>No coding required</span></div></div><div class="orientation"><div><b>01 / PREDICT</b><p>Commit before the animation reveals.</p></div><div><b>02 / TRACE</b><p>Follow vectors, arrows and real numbers.</p></div><div><b>03 / EXPLAIN</b><p>Change one input; explain the effect.</p></div></div><noscript><p class="noscript">The complete lesson, diagrams and worked answers are available below. Enable JavaScript for animations, experiments and automatic answer checking.</p></noscript><section id="opening" class="mission"><span class="eyebrow">TODAY’S MISSION / THE CLOSED NOTEBOOK</span><h2>Can you translate a sentence<br>you are no longer allowed to see?</h2><div class="mission-grid"><div class="cue-card"><p id="memory-sentence">“On Tuesday, Mira sent the small blue parcel to Arun, not to Leela.”</p><button id="hide-source" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="memory-sentence">Close the notebook</button><p>Hide the sentence. Now answer: who must <em>not</em> receive it? What color was the parcel?</p></div><div><ol><li>Build a network that reads one sequence and writes another.</li><li>Make its summary work harder—and expose the bottleneck.</li><li>Invent a way to reopen the notebook for every output.</li><li>Learn two ways to decide where to look.</li></ol></div></div><p class="note">Keep your proposed fix in mind. We will return to it after the first experiment. This human-memory hook is an analogy, not evidence about neural capacity.</p></section><div class="learning-goals"><div><b>Explain the two clocks</b>Separate source reading, target generation and training inputs.</div><div><b>Compute attention</b>Trace scores → weights → context without confusing them.</div><div><b>Compare scorers</b>Distinguish additive and multiplicative computation and ordering.</div></div><p class="bridge">Bring only vectors, addition and multiplication. Lesson 01 introduces sequence state; the foldouts add matrix shapes, softmax and training details. You can follow the main narrative without opening them.</p>'''
for n,x in enumerate(lessons):
 page+=f'''<section class="lab" id="{x['id']}"><div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">{n+1:02d}</span><div><span class="eyebrow">{x['tag']}</span><h2>{x['title']}</h2></div></div><div class="lesson-hook"><span class="step-label">01 / THE HOOK</span><p>{x['hook']}</p><div class="hook-pair"><div class="hook-card"><small>START HERE</small><b>{x['left']}</b></div><div class="hook-card"><small>THE TENSION</small><b>{x['right']}</b></div></div></div><figure class="problem-visual"><span class="step-label">02 / SEE THE PROBLEM</span><div class="problem-flow">'''+''.join(f'<div><b>{j+1:02d}</b><span>{v}</span></div>' for j,v in enumerate(x['problem']))+f'''</div><figcaption>{x['caption']}</figcaption></figure><div class="lesson-explanation"><span class="step-label">03 / EXPLANATION & ANALOGY</span>{x['explain']}</div><div class="formula-card"><span class="step-label">04 / GIVE THE IDEA A RULE</span><div class="equation">{x['formula']}</div><p>{x['legend']}</p></div><details class="theory-fold"><summary><b>Go deeper</b> · {x['deep_title']}</summary><div class="fold-body">{x['deep']}</div></details><div class="interactive-step"><span class="step-label">05 / WATCH → PREDICT → EXPERIMENT</span><div class="animation-lab" data-animation="{x['id']}"><figure class="fallback"><img src="assets/{x['id']}.svg" alt="Numbered {x['tag'].lower()} teaching diagram"><figcaption>Static overview. Enable JavaScript to play the guided walkthrough.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div class="try-this"><b>TRY THIS</b>{x['tryit']}</div><div class="questions-step"><span class="step-label">06 / CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING</span><div class="question-grid">{checks(n*3)}</div></div></section>'''
page+='''<section id="exit" class="lab"><div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">06</span><div><span class="eyebrow">SYNTHESIS / REOPEN THE NOTEBOOK</span><h2>Build the information route.</h2></div></div><p>Return to the parcel sentence. The question changes from “who?” to “what color?” A fixed summary must support both; attention changes which source evidence is retrieved. The output head still has to turn that evidence into an answer.</p>'''+checks(15)+'''<div class="exit-card"><h3>Your design challenge</h3><p>A system translates variable-length medical instructions. It sometimes drops an early negation. Propose an encoder–decoder with attention. Sketch the two clocks and label hᵢ, query, scores, weights, cₜ and vocabulary probabilities. Choose a scorer, give its matrix shapes, explain teacher forcing, and show how padding is excluded. Name one test you would run to check whether the omission actually improves.</p><label for="design-notes">Write your explanation or use this space for lecture notes</label><textarea id="design-notes" placeholder="My information route, scorer, training inputs, mask and evaluation…"></textarea><details class="theory-fold"><summary>Instructor discussion rubric</summary><div class="fold-body"><p>Look for separate source and target clocks; an encoder bank; one source-normalized weight row per output; a context of width Hₑ; an output head over vocabulary; shifted correct targets in training and predictions in inference; zero padding weights; dimensionally valid scoring. Attention offers access but does not guarantee preserving negation. Evaluate held-out negation cases and compare omission rates with a baseline; a pretty heatmap alone is insufficient. For actual medical use, accuracy and clinical validation would need a separate deployment process.</p></div></details></div><div class="export-actions"><button id="export">Export answers & notes ↓</button><button id="reset">Reset saved progress</button><span id="save-status" role="status"></span></div><p class="storage-note">Answers and notes save only in this browser when storage is available. Export makes a portable JSON copy. No data is sent to a server.</p><p class="closing">A decoder need not remember everything at once.<br>It can learn what to look at next.</p></section><section id="sources" class="resources sources"><span class="eyebrow">READING / CREDITS / TEACHING ASSETS</span><h2>Where the ideas come from</h2><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.3215">Sutskever, Vinyals & Le (2014), Sequence to Sequence Learning with Neural Networks</a> — fixed-vector encoder–decoder foundation.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0473">Bahdanau, Cho & Bengio (ICLR 2015), Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align and Translate</a> — learned soft alignment and additive scoring.</li><li><a href="https://aclanthology.org/D15-1166/">Luong, Pham & Manning (EMNLP 2015), Effective Approaches to Attention-based Neural Machine Translation</a> — global/local attention, dot/general/concat scores and input feeding.</li><li><a href="https://github.com/poloclub/transformer-explainer">Polo Club’s Transformer Explainer</a> — inspiration for tracing visible intermediate values and coordinated views. This worksheet implements classical recurrent attention; it does not run GPT-2 or reuse their code or images.</li><li><a href="../transfer-learning/">The collection’s transfer-learning worksheet</a> — inspiration for lecture pacing, prediction stops and foldable detail.</li></ul><p>All diagrams here are original SVG teaching figures generated from explicitly hand-chosen examples. No third-party images or model weights are downloaded. Shared theme and timeline come from this repository’s sequence-modelling worksheet. Translation strings and decoder outputs are scripted; attention scores and contexts are calculated.</p><div class="resource-links"><a class="button" href="assets/gallery.html">Open reusable figures ↗</a><a class="button" href="instructor-guide.md">Instructor guide ↗</a><a class="button" href="storyboard-guide.md">Animation storyboards ↗</a><a class="button" href="../gated-rnns/">Previous: gated RNNs ↗</a></div></section><footer>Deep learning · Lecture presentation & practice sheets · Encoder–decoder and attention</footer></main></div></body></html>'''
